# schedule.zd — a job the deployment runs on a schedule (#18, §14G.4).
#
# THE QUESTION THIS FILE ANSWERS. `timers.zd` shows `every "250ms"` on a
# `client` declaration: a source signal whose writer is the browser's
# scheduler, with no block, because a browser timer never becomes control
# flow. This is the same word one placement to the left, and it is a
# different construct: a job the *deployment* runs, whether or not anybody
# has the page open.
#
# The edit that moves a job from the browser to the deployment is one word,
# on the left-hand side of the declaration, which is the whole reason
# §14G.4 puts a trigger in the `init` slot rather than inventing a
# placement or a second declaration form.
#
# WHY THE BLOCK IS REQUIRED HERE AND FORBIDDEN THERE. A `client` cell lives
# as long as the tab, so a reader can watch it change and everything
# downstream is `from`. A `server` cell lives inside one invocation and is
# discarded when it ends — there is no browser attached to a beat, so a
# schedule with nothing under it computes a timestamp nobody can ever read.
# The block is the reader. That is also what makes three of §14G.4's rules
# structural instead of stated: one handler per trigger, because a
# declaration has one block; a trigger with no handler is unwritable; and
# no handler is attached to a *change*, so the graph cannot self-excite.
#
# WHAT THE CELL HOLDS. `every` on a `server` declaration requires `Whole`,
# and the value is the beat's scheduled start time in seconds since 1970 —
# when it was *due*, not when the platform got to it, so a skipped beat is
# visible as a jump larger than the cadence. A beat *count* would need
# somewhere to keep the count, and a `server` placement does not declare
# storage.
#
# THE CADENCE IS ONE OF NINETEEN. `"1m"` through `"30m"`, `"1h"` through
# `"12h"`, and `"1d"`: the durations that divide their unit. That is not
# tidiness. A cron expression cannot say "every seven minutes" — `*/7`
# steps 0, 7, ... 56 and then jumps back — while AWS's `rate(7 minutes)`
# genuinely can, so accepting `"7m"` would mean this program meaning two
# different things depending on `--target`.
#
# WHAT RUNS IT. `zdc deploy --target cloudflare` writes the cron rule into
# `wrangler.toml` and a `scheduled()` export into `worker.js`. The other
# three targets **refuse this program**, each naming the platform fact that
# stops it — Lambda's entry shim, Vercel's cron-is-an-HTTP-route, and
# `Deno.cron`'s absence from the platform the adapter targets. A job that
# was written out and never scheduled would be a failure nothing reports.
#
# WHAT IS NOT HERE. `inbound "stripe/payment"` — the other trigger §14G.4
# designs, a signal an outside request writes. It is reserved in the
# grammar and refused by name: `zdc explain E0107`.

state visits is durable Whole starting 0

state digest is durable List of Text starting []

# The job. Nothing reads `hourly` except the block under it, and that is
# the ordinary case: the cell exists so the beat has somewhere to land.
state hourly is server Whole every "1h"
    add 1 to visits
    append "checked" to digest

view title is "Scheduled"
    Column
        Text "This page is rendered by the browser."
        Text "The counter beside it is advanced by the deployment, hourly,"
        Text "with nobody watching."
        when visits
            Loading
                Text "counting"
            Failed with problem
                Text problem.message
            Ready with count
                Text text of count
